Here's an example of a TypeScript interface for a Rectangle:

```typescript
interface Rectangle {
  width: number;
  height: number;
}
```

In this example, the `Rectangle` interface defines two properties: `width` and `height`, both of which are required to be of type `number`.

When you create a class or object that implements this interface, you must ensure it has the specified properties:

```typescript
class MyRectangle implements Rectangle {
  width: number;
  height: number;

  constructor(width: number, height: number) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }
}
```

In TypeScript, interfaces help ensure that your code is more type-safe and easier to understand, as they clearly define the expected structure of your objects and classes. This can help catch errors during development and make your code more maintainable in the long run.

